BLOCKING

B3 · Nine of W-020's twelve brief behaviours have no red of their own. reds
     01-08 and 12 are one byte-identical document, stored nine times.

     With the `# behaviour:` line stripped, 01,02,03,04,05,06,07,08,12 all
     hash f3ea7c22a6b2. The single document is:

       SyntaxError: The requested module './lifecycle.js' does not provide
       an export named 'runDone'

     A module that fails to load proves that nothing existed yet. It does not
     show behaviour 5 failing, and it cannot be told apart from behaviour 3
     failing, because no assertion in either ran. 09, 10, 11 and 13-16 are the
     counter-example: seven distinct logs, each naming its own assertions.

     I am not raising a new standard. This is the standard this cascade set,
     one round ago, on the sibling opus, in my own hand. W-021's round-3
     review (ci/W-021-review-3.log, B1) found precisely this artifact — "all
     twelve reds were one of two byte-identical ERR_MODULE_NOT_FOUND
     documents ... no assertion ever ran" — and made it BLOCKING. builder-b
     discharged it in 113f20e by re-running each behaviour slice against a
     stubbed worktree, and W-021's store now hashes twelve distinct logs.

     The identical defect in W-020 was seen in round 2 (F2) and round 3 (F4)
     and carried as advisory both times. Round 3 had a reason: it believed
     recapture was impossible. That reason is gone. The method is proven twice
     over in this cascade, and builder-a applied it himself — in ae22e88, to
     behaviours 9, 10, 11 and 16 — without applying it to 01-08 and 12 in the
     same pass. What remains is not an impossibility, it is nine files that
     were not re-run.

     Engineering lex §7 asks a `done` item for "the recorded red for every
     behaviour its spec listed". Nine copies of one module-load failure
     answer that in letter and not in substance. W-020 is the opus that ships
     the red store and defines what a red is; if it closes with a store that
     fails the standard its sibling was held to one commit earlier,
     opus.red_evidence is decorative from birth — the rule counts files and
     reads one header, so nine identical documents satisfy it exactly as well
     as nine real ones. The rule cannot catch this. A reviewer can.

     This is a reviewer's call with a mechanical remedy, not a Patron
     disposition. Round 5 is one commit: re-run lifecycle.test.ts's slices for
     behaviours 1-8 and 12 against a stubbed worktree, exactly as 113f20e and
     ae22e88 already do, and record them through `red --cwd`. Keep the script
     this time (CLAUDE.md: mechanical work is a script you write, run and
     keep) — neither ae22e88's nor 113f20e's harness survives, so neither
     reconstruction can be audited or repeated.

     One caution for whoever does it. ae22e88's no-op stub leaves the
     silence-only assertions trivially true: 10.log's "no directory created
     for a passing command" and 11.log's "none of the usage refusals created
     ci/reds" both read PASS, because a stub that does nothing creates nothing.
     W-021's review named this exact trap and builder-b avoided it by
     stubbing to a value that fails rather than to nothing. Do that here.


